<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Sugambri</h1><p class="st-meta">55 BCE – 8 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A warlike Germanic people of the lower Rhine who repeatedly defied Rome until Augustus deported much of the tribe across the river into Gaul.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Sugambri lived on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sieg-river">Sieg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippe-river">Lippe</a>. They first appear in 55 BCE, when they sheltered survivors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usipetes">Usipetes</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tencteri">Tencteri</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> bridged the Rhine and ravaged their land in retaliation.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of their internal organisation beyond war-leaders and a reputation for raiding. In 16 BCE they crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usipetes">Usipetes</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tencteri">Tencteri</a> and defeated the Roman legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-lollius">Marcus Lollius</a>, capturing the eagle of the Fifth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, an event known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clades-lolliana">clades Lolliana</a>. This defeat helped prompt Augustus' German campaigns.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Drusus</a> attacked them from 12 BCE, and in 8 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> forcibly resettled tens of thousands on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, where they became the Cugerni. Those who stayed merged into later groups, and the name was later absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Frankish confederation</a>; Frankish kings were poetically called Sicambri.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sugambri.jpg" alt="Sugambri" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Rhine_10BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>55 BCE – 8 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maelo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Weser-Rhine Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sicambri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-32&amp;focus=sugambri" aria-label="Sugambri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 394 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sugambri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 650h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5237 650h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=-32&amp;focus=sugambri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

